WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.380
How do you measure how hard a cell sticks, pulls, or travels?

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.900
A still picture only shows where cells sit.

3
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.460
Living cell assays turn invisible grip, force, and travel into direct measurements.

4
00:00:16.417 --> 00:00:17.597
Start with sticking.

5
00:00:18.937 --> 00:00:23.277
Separate cells and watch if they clump back together: a reaggregation assay.

6
00:00:24.697 --> 00:00:29.977
Every anchoring grip uses four links: outer partner, receptor, internal adaptors,

7
00:00:30.297 --> 00:00:35.857
and inner cable. To test a link, remove or block it, watch clumping change, then

8
00:00:35.857 --> 00:00:36.857
restore it.

9
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:42.070
Sticking also powers crawling: protrusion, grip, contraction, and release.

10
00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:48.970
Integrin receptors and linker proteins form focal adhesions, anchoring cells to

11
00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:54.110
their substrate. Filament growth is the engine, and adhesion is the clutch.

12
00:00:55.790 --> 00:01:00.590
Mechanics assays measure traction stress, force per area on the substrate, alongside

13
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:01.950
local actin speed.

14
00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:07.387
Inside developing tissue, biologists track where living cells travel.

15
00:01:08.587 --> 00:01:12.467
A dye like DiI or DiA dissolves into cell membranes.

16
00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.787
Cells leaving that spot carry it along.

17
00:01:17.247 --> 00:01:20.507
Slices let you watch cells move; grafts show what they become.

18
00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:25.707
Each assay measures one thing directly: grip, pull, or travel.

19
00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.190
Back to the crawling cell.

20
00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.750
Focal adhesions anchor its forward pull.

21
00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:35.390
Suppose you make those adhesions much stronger.

22
00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:41.550
Will the cell always crawl faster, or can stronger adhesion slow migration down?

23
00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:52.753
Stronger adhesion can slow migration, because the rear must let go, and stronger

24
00:01:52.753 --> 00:01:54.133
grip resists that release.

25
00:01:55.573 --> 00:02:01.353
In PtK1 cells, traction stress and local actin speed had a two-part relationship.

26
00:02:02.633 --> 00:02:05.433
It changed near 8 to 10 nm/s.

27
00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:11.533
So faster flow does not always mean more traction; the clutch is only a starting

28
00:02:11.533 --> 00:02:12.533
model.

29
00:02:12.917 --> 00:02:14.637
Every assay has limits.

30
00:02:16.017 --> 00:02:19.397
Reaggregation tests clumping, not force on one contact.

31
00:02:20.897 --> 00:02:24.417
Dye tracking follows moving cells, but does not show what they become.

32
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:29.157
When losing a gene halts migration, two ideas compete.

33
00:02:30.437 --> 00:02:33.977
It may be needed in migrating cells, or in the tissue they cross.

34
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.260
These assays unlocked discoveries.

35
00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:45.380
In 1992, Hirano and colleagues studied PC9 cells carrying the receptor E-cadherin.

36
00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:51.480
Calcium-dependent aggregation was greatly reduced, and cells grew mostly singly.

37
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.160
Putting the adaptor α-catenin back restored firm, compact aggregates.

38
00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.060
Binding outside is not enough for strong adhesion.

39
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:08.353
In 1996, de Carlos, López-Mascaraque and Valverde dyed the lateral ganglionic

40
00:03:08.353 --> 00:03:12.793
eminence, a ventral forebrain region, in cultured rat embryos.

41
00:03:14.353 --> 00:03:18.273
Most labelled cells settled nearby in ventrolateral cortex.

42
00:03:19.753 --> 00:03:24.733
Others ran sideways, tangentially, into the preplate, the first layer of young

43
00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:25.733
cortical neurons.

44
00:03:26.817 --> 00:03:31.757
In 1999, Wichterle, Alvarez-Buylla and colleagues tracked cells from another

45
00:03:31.757 --> 00:03:33.677
eminence, the MGE.

46
00:03:35.117 --> 00:03:40.857
In embryonic slices, those cells migrated extensively toward the cortex, and grafted

47
00:03:40.857 --> 00:03:42.177
cells became neurons.

48
00:03:43.637 --> 00:03:48.097
Each assay captures one living step, and shows what still needs another test.
